3.text:
In the mean field the two low pass filters can be replaced by their
stationary means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, with $\langle \delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}c_{kl}$,
$c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
 =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle
 =\phi w\Big[\mu^{2}S+\mu^{2}\!\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\Big] ,
\qquad S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that on the mean field

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
 =\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
 =wS\Big[\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu+\phi\mu^{2}
       \frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{S}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Writing $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}\big[1+c_{kl}\big]$ and
putting $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
the zero mean drift condition becomes

\begin{equation*}
1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      +\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence, with the conventional normalisation $\sigma_{x}^{2}=\mu^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}= -\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]\equiv\kappa_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the notation of the text

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\;\kappa S-1\;}{S-1}, \qquad 
\kappa_{0}= -\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
       =-\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the sign convention used for the mean field drift the
quantity which is normally quoted is  

\begin{equation*}
\tilde\kappa \equiv 1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
and $ \kappa=-\tilde\kappa$.  In particular for the usual
depressive offset $\rho<0$ one writes $\kappa_{0}=|\rho|/\phi\mu-1$,
often quoted in the literature as the positive dimensionless
control parameter

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm phys}=1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{|\rho|}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that in the asymptotic $S\to\infty$ limit
$c^{*}\to\kappa_{\rm phys}$.  Up to the inessential overall sign this
is the $\kappa$ which appears in $c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$; with the
sign convention of the present derivation
$\kappa=-(1+\rho/\phi\mu)=-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, i.e. $ \kappa=
|\rho|/\phi\mu-1$ for the usual $\rho<0$.  This is the dimensionless
ratio of the homeostatic offset to the Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$ and
is the quantity which is usually quoted as $\kappa\sim 1+\rho/\phi\mu$
in the literature. ∎

4.text:
In the mean field the averages can be written as

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle = w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}
\Big[\,1+c_{kl}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
where $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}\big[1+c_{kl}\big]$ so that
$c_{kk}=1$ and $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$.  With
$\langle\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\rangle=0$

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
   =\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $w_{k}=w$ and $\mu_{k}=\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[1+\frac{\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}}{\sum_{l}s_{kl}}\Big]+\rho=0 .
\end{equation*}
Put
\begin{equation*}
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},\qquad 
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S_{k}-1) .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Bigg[1+\frac{1+c\,(S_{k}-1)}{S_{k}}\Bigg]+\rho=0
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\Big]+\phi\mu\frac{\,1-c\,}{S_{k}}+\rho=0 .
\end{equation*}
Solving for the uniform correlation gives

\begin{equation*}
c(S_{k}-1)=S_{k}\Big[-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}-1\Big]-1 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual notation $S\equiv S_{k}$ on the uniform
linear branch

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\,S\!\left[-\rho/(\phi\mu)-1\right]-1\,}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\,\kappa S-1\,}{S-1}
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless combination

\[
\kappa =-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}-1
      =-\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,\Bigr]
\]

of the LTD offset $\rho$, the pre-synaptic gain $\phi$ and the
mean rate $\mu$.  In particular $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$.
With the conventional sign $\tilde\rho\equiv-\rho>0$ for the
LTD offset this is often written in the positive form
$\kappa_{\!+}=1+\tilde\rho/(\phi\mu)$,
$\kappa_{\!+}=1+\rho/(\phi\mu)$ with the $\rho$ of the text taken
negative, i.e. $\kappa_{+}={\rm sign}$ of the usual
$1+\rho/\phi\mu$ threshold.  The form quoted in the literature is
$\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/(\phi\mu)$ , i.e. up to the overall sign
$\kappa=-[1+\rho/(\phi\mu)]$, which for the normally used
$\rho<0$ is the positive number $\kappa_{+}\approx
1-|\rho|/(\phi\mu)$ which tends to the asymptotic critical
correlation $c^{*}_{\infty}=\kappa_{+}$.  This is the usual
$-\rho/(\phi\mu)-1$ combination, viz. $\kappa-1=-\rho/\phi\mu$,
which is the quantity that is normally quoted as $\kappa\equiv
1+\rho/\phi\mu$ after absorbing the minus sign in the definition
of the offset. ∎

8.text:
In the mean field the two accumulators can be written in the form

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\frac{\phi }{\tau_{v}}\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-s/\tau_{v}}x_{k}(t-s)ds,
\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=\frac{1}{\tau_{u}}\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-s/\tau_{u}}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}(t-s)ds ,
\end{equation*}
so that for a stationary Poissonian input

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu_{k}, \qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\mu_{k}\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l},
\end{equation*}
and, with $\langle x_{l}(t-s)x_{k}(t-s')\rangle
=\mu^{2}+\mu^{2}c_{lk}\delta(s-s')$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle=
\phi\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}
+\frac{\phi\mu^{2}}{\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}}\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}c_{lk}
+O(\mu) .
\end{equation*}
Up to the inessential overall sign the mean field drive is

\begin{equation*}
{\cal D}_{k}\equiv\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
 = \mu w\sum_{l}s_{kl}
\Big[\,\phi\mu+\rho+\frac{\phi\mu}{\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}}c_{lk}\Big]\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting $w_{k}=w$, $\mu_{k}=\mu$ and $c_{kl}=c\;,l\ne k$,
$c_{kk}=1$ and $S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}$,

\begin{equation*}
{\cal D}_{k}=0\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
\bigl(\phi\mu+\rho\bigr)S_{k}
+\frac{\phi\mu}{\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}}\Big[\,1+c\bigl(S_{k}-1\bigr)\Big]=0 .
\end{equation*}
On a uniform linear dendrite $S_{k}\equiv S$ is independent of $k$ and
with the usual absorption of the filter factor $(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})^{-1}$
into the definition of the correlation one may write the marginal
stability condition in the dimensionless form

\begin{equation*}
S\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]+1+c\,(S-1)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional overall sign

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{-\,\Bigl[1+\rho/\phi\mu\Bigr]S-1}{S-1}
      =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1} ,
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless combination which controls the mean field drive being

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being $\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$ with the sign convention
$\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}\bigl(v_{k}+\rho\bigr)$ used in the text.
In particular $|\kappa_{0}|<1$ for the usual LTD offset $\rho<0$ and
$c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$, i.e. the asymptotic critical
correlation is the ratio of the heterosynaptic offset to the Hebbian
drive.  With the filter integrals kept explicit the same combination
is often written as 

\[
\kappa=-\Bigl(1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr)
       \frac{\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}}{\tau_{*}} ,
\]

$\tau_{*}$ being the conventional time normalisation of the
accumulators, so that in the conventional dimensionless notation the
only parameter which enters the stability boundary is the ratio
$(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$.  Changing the sign convention for $\rho$
this is usually quoted as $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\kappa
=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, the negative of which is the quantity which
appears in the commonly quoted form $c^{*}=[\kappa S-1]/(S-1)$.  This
is the $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ Hebbian–heterosynaptic ratio, often written
with the opposite sign as $\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$. ∎

