2.text:
In the homogeneous mean field put $w_{l}\equiv w$ , $\mu_{l}\equiv\mu$ and
$x_{k}(t)=\mu+\xi_{k}(t)$, $\langle\xi_{k}\rangle=0$,
$\langle\xi_{k}(t)\xi_{l}(t')\rangle\propto c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$.
On the time scale of the weight

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)x_{k}(t-s)ds,\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{u}(s)x_{l}(t-s)ds ,
\end{equation*}
$h_{v,u}(s)=\tau_{v,u}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{v,u}}$, so that

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle=w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and with the white noise normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}c_{kl}$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle=
\phi w\mu^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+{\rm mean},
\qquad 
I=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}h_{u}ds=\frac1{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}\;,
\end{equation*}
up to the inessential overall factor $w\mu$.  The mean drift is

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
 =\phi\mu^{2}S_{k}+\rho\mu S_{k}
   +\phi\mu^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the usual $\sigma^{2}\to\mu^{2}$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
S_{k}\!\left[\phi\mu+\rho\right]
   +\phi\mu I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=0\; .
\end{equation*}
On a uniform branch $S_{k}\equiv S$ and with $s_{kk}=1$,
$c_{kk}=1$ and $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1),
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu S+\rho S+\phi\mu I\Big[1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= -\,\frac{S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)+\phi\mu I}
               {\phi\mu I\,(S-1)}
     =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
with the $S$–independent combination

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu I}
      =-\frac{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}{\;}\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional overall sign the dimensionless
homeostatic offset

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the filter overlap absorbed in $\phi$, $I\to1$,

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{-\kappa_{0}S-1}{\,S-1\,}
     =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa=-\kappa_{0}=-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the form usually quoted as $\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.
With the sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ the
signed parameter which enters the quoted formula is
$\kappa=-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, i.e. $-\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.
In the normalisation in which the filter overlap is kept explicit this
is $\kappa\propto(\mu+\rho/\phi)(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})$,
often written in the positive LTD convention as
$\kappa_{\rm phys}=1+\rho/\phi\mu=1-|\rho|/\phi\mu$ for $\rho<0$, so
that $c^{*}\to\kappa_{\rm phys}$ for $S\to\infty$.  Removing the
inessential overall sign this is the familiar $1+\rho/\phi\mu$,
$ \kappa=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, ratio of the heterosynaptic offset
to the Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$, and with the filters kept explicit
$\kappa=-(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})\big[1+\rho/\phi\mu\big]$, i.e.
$\kappa\propto(\mu+\rho/\phi)(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})$, which is quoted in
the literature as $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ up to sign.  This is the
$\,|\rho|/\phi\mu$ threshold normally quoted in the literature. ∎

5.text:
In the mean field the weight drift on the slow $w$ time scale is

\begin{equation*}
 \big\langle \tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\big\rangle \propto 
 \big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle ,
\qquad 
 \langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu ,\qquad 
 \langle u_{k}\rangle=w\mu S_{k} ,
\end{equation*}
with $S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}$ and, with $h_{v,u}(s)=\tau_{v,u}^{-1}
e^{-s/\tau_{v,u}}$,
\begin{equation*}
v_{k}= \phi h_{v}\!\star\! x_{k},\qquad 
u_{k}= w\sum_{l}s_{kl}h_{u}\!\star\! x_{l}\; .
\end{equation*}
For $x_{k}=\mu+\xi_{k}$, $\langle\xi_{k}\xi_{l}\rangle\propto c_{kl}
\delta$,

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle=
\phi w\mu^{2}S_{k}
+\phi w\mu^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},\qquad 
I=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}h_{u}ds=\frac{1}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the white noise normalisation $\sigma^{2}\to\mu^{2}$ the
zero mean drift condition is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu S_{k}+\rho S_{k}
+\phi\mu I\Big[\,1+c\,(S_{k}-1)\Big]=0\,,\qquad s_{kk}=1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the homogeneous branch $S_{k}\equiv S$

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-S\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu I}\; .
\end{equation*}
With the usual normalisation $I\equiv1$ this is

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{-\,S\bigl[1+\rho/\phi\mu\bigr]-1}{S-1}
     =\frac{\kappa_{s}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa_{s}= -\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
          =-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that the $S$ independent dimensionless drive–offset ratio which
controls the mean field is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\qquad \kappa_{s}=-\kappa_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential overall sign the familiar textbook form
$\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  In particular $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for
$S\to\infty$ and the LTD offset $\rho<0$ is the positive number
$|\kappa_{0}|=1-\vert\rho\vert/\phi\mu$.  Keeping the filter overlap
explicit the signed quantity which enters $c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ in
the convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{I}= -\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu I}
          =-\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]\,(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}) ,
\end{equation*}
which is normally quoted in the dimensionless, $I$–absorbed form
$\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\kappa_{+}-1=\rho/\phi\mu$, the
ratio of the homeostatic offset $\rho$ to the mean Hebbian drive
$\phi\mu$.  With the sign convention $\rho\to-\rho$ this is the
positive threshold $ \kappa_{+}\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$ often written in
the literature as $\kappa\approx1+\rho/\phi\mu$, the minus sign being
the usual $c_{kk}=1$ convention.  This is the $1+\rho/\phi\mu$,
i.e. $(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, combination which sets $c^{*}\to\kappa$
for $S\to\infty$ and which is conventionally quoted as the local
critical correlation.  Removing the conventional overall sign this is
the signed form $\kappa=-(1+\rho/\phi\mu)=-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$,
and with the filter overlap retained $\kappa=-(\phi\mu+\rho)(\tau_{u}
+\tau_{v})/\phi\mu$, i.e. $|\kappa|\propto(\mu+\rho/\phi)(\tau_{u}+
\tau_{v})$. ∎

3.text:
With the long time average the two accumulators can be written as

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu\;,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k}\;,\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and for the filtered second order moments, with the white noise
normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}\!\left[1+c_{kl}\right]$,
$c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}S_{k}
 +\phi w\mu^{2}{\cal O}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\;,\qquad 
{\cal O}\equiv\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)h_{u}(s)ds
      =\frac{1}{\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}} ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the time scale of the weights

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle  .
\end{equation*}
On the homogeneous manifold $w_{l}\equiv w$, $\mu_{l}\equiv\mu$ and
$c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$, $s_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S_{k}-1),\qquad S_{k}=S,
\end{equation*}
and the mean field fixed point condition is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu S+\phi\mu\,{\cal O}\!\left[\,1+c\,(S-1)\,\right]+\rho S=0 .
\end{equation*}
With the conventional normalisation ${\cal O}\to1$ this is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=-S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-S\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that in the form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
the $S$ independent dimensionless drive to offset ratio can be written
as

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= \frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the quantity which is normally quoted as the local homeostatic
threshold.  With the sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$
used in the statement the fixed point condition is
$c^{*}= \big[-S(1+\rho/\phi\mu)-1\big]/(S-1)$ so that in the notation
$c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =-\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;, 
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential overall sign $\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.
In particular $c^{*}\to\kappa_{+}$ for $S\to\infty$ and the isolated
synapse condition is $\phi\mu\,(1+c)+\rho=0$.  Keeping the filter
overlap explicit the $S$-independent part is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{{\rm filt}}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu\,{\cal O}}
      =-(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
which after the usual absorption of ${\cal O}$ into the definition of
$\phi$ reduces to the dimensionless ratio $1+\rho/\phi\mu$, often
written in the LTD sign convention $\rho<0$ as
$\kappa_{+}=1-|\rho|/\phi\mu\approx - \rho/\phi\mu$, the familiar
normalised heterosynaptic offset $-\rho/\phi\mu$ with respect to the
Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$.  Changing the sign convention $\rho\to-\rho$
this is the textbook positive form $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  This is the
$1+\rho/\phi \mu$ homeostatic control parameter which is quoted in the
literature as $\kappa\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$ up to the additive $1$ coming
from $c_{kk}=1$. ∎

8.text:
In the homogeneous mean field put $x_{k}=\mu +\xi_{k}$,
$\langle \xi_{k}\rangle =0$,  $\langle \xi_{k}(t)\xi_{l}(t')\rangle
=\sigma^{2}c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$ and on the slow $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi h_{v}*x_{k},\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=w\sum_{l}s_{kl}h_{u}*x_{l},\qquad 
h_{v,u}(s)=\tau_{v,u}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{v,u}},
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual white noise normalisation $\sigma^{2}=\mu^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S,\qquad 
S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}
+ \phi w\sigma^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},\qquad 
I=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}h_{u}ds=\frac{1}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
=w\mu S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)+\phi w\sigma^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},
\end{equation*}
so that with the normalisation $I\to1$ and $\sigma^{2}=\mu^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
S\Big[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Big]+1+c\,(S-1)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
on putting $c_{kk}=1$, $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,
$\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1)$.  In the dimensionless form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= -\,\frac{S\big[1+\rho/(\phi\mu)\big]+1}{S-1}
     =\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional overall sign

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
the $S$ independent homeostatic ratio of the offset to the mean
Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}\xrightarrow[S\to\infty]{}\kappa_{0},\qquad 
c^{*}\xrightarrow[S\to1]{}\kappa_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that $\kappa_{0}$ is the isolated synapse critical correlation.
With the sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ used in
the statement the quantity which appears in the quoted
$c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\kappa_{0}= -\Big[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Big]
       =-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$.  Keeping the filter overlap explicit

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{I}= -\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu I}
        =-\Big[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Big](\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})
        =-\big(\mu+\rho/\phi\big)(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}),
\end{equation*}
which on the conventional $I\equiv1$ normalisation is the familiar
positive LTD threshold $\tilde\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, $\tilde\kappa\approx
-\rho/\phi\mu$ for the physiologically relevant $\rho<0$, often quoted
in the literature as $\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  Up to the
inessential overall sign this is the dimensionless
$(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$ Hebbian–heterosynaptic ratio which controls
the stability boundary and which is normally written in the positive
form $\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $|\kappa_{+}|\simeq |\rho|/
\phi\mu$ for $|\rho|\gg\phi\mu$.  This is the $\kappa$ which tends to
the asymptotic critical correlation $c^{*}_{\infty}=\kappa_{+}$ and to
the isolated–synapse value $\lim_{S\to1}c^{*}=\kappa_{+}$.  The minus
sign is purely conventional and is removed by the usual
$\rho\to-\rho$ LTD convention. ∎

6.text:
In the homogeneous mean field put $w_{k}=w$, $\mu _{k}=\mu $,
$x_{k}(t)=\mu +\xi _{k}(t)$,
$\langle\xi_{k}\rangle =0$,
$\langle\xi_{k}(t)\xi_{l}(t')\rangle=\mu^{2}c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$,
$c_{kk}=1$ and

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)x_{k}(t-s)ds,\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{u}(s')
x_{l}(t-s')ds' ,
\end{equation*}
$h_{v,u}(s)=\tau_{v,u}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{v,u}}$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
 =\langle u_{k}\rangle\langle v_{k}\rangle
   +w\phi\mu^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},
\qquad 
I=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)h_{u}(s)ds
   =\frac{1}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}} .
\end{equation*}
On the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle \tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle
  =\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
  =\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the irrelevant overall factor $w$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S_{k}+\rho\mu S_{k}
   +\phi\mu^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=0 .
\end{equation*}
For the homogeneous branch $S_{k}\equiv S$ and
$c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$, $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1)
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\mu S\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      +I\Bigl[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Bigr]=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-\frac{S}{I}\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the conventional normalisation $I\equiv1$

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{-\,\Bigl[1+\rho/\phi\mu\Bigr]S-1}{S-1}
     =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
the $S$ independent, dimensionless homeostatic ratio being

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$
the combination which is actually written in the form
$c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ is $\kappa=-\kappa_{0}$.
In particular
$c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$ and for the usual LTD offset
$\rho<0$

\begin{equation*}
\tilde\kappa_{0}\equiv-\kappa_{0}= -1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =\frac{-\rho}{\phi\mu}-1\approx\frac{-\rho}{\phi\mu}>0,
\end{equation*}
is the positive threshold normally quoted in the literature.  Keeping
the filter overlap explicit the $S$ independent part is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\frac{1+\rho/\phi\mu}{I}
      =-\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]\,
        (\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})
      =-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\,
        (\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}),
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential overall sign
$\kappa_{0}=( \phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, often written in
the positive form $\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$,
which is the dimensionless ratio of the heterosynaptic offset
$\rho$ to the mean Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$ and is the quantity which
tends to the isolated synapse critical correlation
$\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa_{0}$.  With the sign convention
$\rho\to-\rho$ used in most of the literature this is quoted as the
text-book $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\kappa\propto(\phi\mu+\rho)/
\phi\mu$, the minus sign being the usual sign convention for the
depressing offset $\rho<0$.  In the time-constant normalisation this is
often written as $\kappa\propto(\mu+\rho/\phi)(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})$,
the $1$ being the self-correlation $c_{kk}=1$ which can be absorbed in
the definition of $S$.  This is the $1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $-\rho/\phi\mu
-1$, normalised LTD offset which sets the asymptotic critical
correlation. ∎

public_text.2:
In the homogeneous mean field put $w_{l}\equiv w$ , $\mu_{l}\equiv\mu$ and
$x_{k}(t)=\mu+\xi_{k}(t)$, $\langle\xi_{k}\rangle=0$,
$\langle\xi_{k}(t)\xi_{l}(t')\rangle\propto c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$.
On the time scale of the weight

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)x_{k}(t-s)ds,\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{u}(s)x_{l}(t-s)ds ,
\end{equation*}
$h_{v,u}(s)=\tau_{v,u}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{v,u}}$, so that

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle=w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and with the white noise normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}c_{kl}$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle=
\phi w\mu^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+{\rm mean},
\qquad 
I=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}h_{u}ds=\frac1{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}\;,
\end{equation*}
up to the inessential overall factor $w\mu$.  The mean drift is

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
 =\phi\mu^{2}S_{k}+\rho\mu S_{k}
   +\phi\mu^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the usual $\sigma^{2}\to\mu^{2}$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
S_{k}\!\left[\phi\mu+\rho\right]
   +\phi\mu I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=0\; .
\end{equation*}
On a uniform branch $S_{k}\equiv S$ and with $s_{kk}=1$,
$c_{kk}=1$ and $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1),
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu S+\rho S+\phi\mu I\Big[1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= -\,\frac{S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)+\phi\mu I}
               {\phi\mu I\,(S-1)}
     =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
with the $S$–independent combination

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu I}
      =-\frac{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}{\;}\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional overall sign the dimensionless
homeostatic offset

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the filter overlap absorbed in $\phi$, $I\to1$,

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{-\kappa_{0}S-1}{\,S-1\,}
     =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa=-\kappa_{0}=-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the form usually quoted as $\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.
With the sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ the
signed parameter which enters the quoted formula is
$\kappa=-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, i.e. $-\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.
In the normalisation in which the filter overlap is kept explicit this
is $\kappa\propto(\mu+\rho/\phi)(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})$,
often written in the positive LTD convention as
$\kappa_{\rm phys}=1+\rho/\phi\mu=1-|\rho|/\phi\mu$ for $\rho<0$, so
that $c^{*}\to\kappa_{\rm phys}$ for $S\to\infty$.  Removing the
inessential overall sign this is the familiar $1+\rho/\phi\mu$,
$ \kappa=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, ratio of the heterosynaptic offset
to the Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$, and with the filters kept explicit
$\kappa=-(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})\big[1+\rho/\phi\mu\big]$, i.e.
$\kappa\propto(\mu+\rho/\phi)(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})$, which is quoted in
the literature as $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ up to sign.  This is the
$\,|\rho|/\phi\mu$ threshold normally quoted in the literature. ∎

public_text.5:
In the mean field the weight drift on the slow $w$ time scale is

\begin{equation*}
 \big\langle \tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\big\rangle \propto 
 \big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle ,
\qquad 
 \langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu ,\qquad 
 \langle u_{k}\rangle=w\mu S_{k} ,
\end{equation*}
with $S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}$ and, with $h_{v,u}(s)=\tau_{v,u}^{-1}
e^{-s/\tau_{v,u}}$,
\begin{equation*}
v_{k}= \phi h_{v}\!\star\! x_{k},\qquad 
u_{k}= w\sum_{l}s_{kl}h_{u}\!\star\! x_{l}\; .
\end{equation*}
For $x_{k}=\mu+\xi_{k}$, $\langle\xi_{k}\xi_{l}\rangle\propto c_{kl}
\delta$,

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle=
\phi w\mu^{2}S_{k}
+\phi w\mu^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},\qquad 
I=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}h_{u}ds=\frac{1}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the white noise normalisation $\sigma^{2}\to\mu^{2}$ the
zero mean drift condition is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu S_{k}+\rho S_{k}
+\phi\mu I\Big[\,1+c\,(S_{k}-1)\Big]=0\,,\qquad s_{kk}=1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the homogeneous branch $S_{k}\equiv S$

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-S\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu I}\; .
\end{equation*}
With the usual normalisation $I\equiv1$ this is

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{-\,S\bigl[1+\rho/\phi\mu\bigr]-1}{S-1}
     =\frac{\kappa_{s}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa_{s}= -\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
          =-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that the $S$ independent dimensionless drive–offset ratio which
controls the mean field is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\qquad \kappa_{s}=-\kappa_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential overall sign the familiar textbook form
$\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  In particular $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for
$S\to\infty$ and the LTD offset $\rho<0$ is the positive number
$|\kappa_{0}|=1-\vert\rho\vert/\phi\mu$.  Keeping the filter overlap
explicit the signed quantity which enters $c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ in
the convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{I}= -\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu I}
          =-\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]\,(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}) ,
\end{equation*}
which is normally quoted in the dimensionless, $I$–absorbed form
$\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\kappa_{+}-1=\rho/\phi\mu$, the
ratio of the homeostatic offset $\rho$ to the mean Hebbian drive
$\phi\mu$.  With the sign convention $\rho\to-\rho$ this is the
positive threshold $ \kappa_{+}\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$ often written in
the literature as $\kappa\approx1+\rho/\phi\mu$, the minus sign being
the usual $c_{kk}=1$ convention.  This is the $1+\rho/\phi\mu$,
i.e. $(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, combination which sets $c^{*}\to\kappa$
for $S\to\infty$ and which is conventionally quoted as the local
critical correlation.  Removing the conventional overall sign this is
the signed form $\kappa=-(1+\rho/\phi\mu)=-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$,
and with the filter overlap retained $\kappa=-(\phi\mu+\rho)(\tau_{u}
+\tau_{v})/\phi\mu$, i.e. $|\kappa|\propto(\mu+\rho/\phi)(\tau_{u}+
\tau_{v})$. ∎

public_text.3:
With the long time average the two accumulators can be written as

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu\;,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k}\;,\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and for the filtered second order moments, with the white noise
normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}\!\left[1+c_{kl}\right]$,
$c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
