as

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= \frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the quantity which is normally quoted as the local homeostatic
threshold.  With the sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$
used in the statement the fixed point condition is
$c^{*}= \big[-S(1+\rho/\phi\mu)-1\big]/(S-1)$ so that in the notation
$c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =-\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;, 
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential overall sign $\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.
In particular $c^{*}\to\kappa_{+}$ for $S\to\infty$ and the isolated
synapse condition is $\phi\mu\,(1+c)+\rho=0$.  Keeping the filter
overlap explicit the $S$-independent part is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{{\rm filt}}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu\,{\cal O}}
      =-(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
which after the usual absorption of ${\cal O}$ into the definition of
$\phi$ reduces to the dimensionless ratio $1+\rho/\phi\mu$, often
written in the LTD sign convention $\rho<0$ as
$\kappa_{+}=1-|\rho|/\phi\mu\approx - \rho/\phi\mu$, the familiar
normalised heterosynaptic offset $-\rho/\phi\mu$ with respect to the
Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$.  Changing the sign convention $\rho\to-\rho$
this is the textbook positive form $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  This is the
$1+\rho/\phi \mu$ homeostatic control parameter which is quoted in the
literature as $\kappa\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$ up to the additive $1$ coming
from $c_{kk}=1$. ∎

public_text.8:
In the homogeneous mean field put $x_{k}=\mu +\xi_{k}$,
$\langle \xi_{k}\rangle =0$,  $\langle \xi_{k}(t)\xi_{l}(t')\rangle
=\sigma^{2}c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$ and on the slow $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi h_{v}*x_{k},\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=w\sum_{l}s_{kl}h_{u}*x_{l},\qquad 
h_{v,u}(s)=\tau_{v,u}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{v,u}},
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual white noise normalisation $\sigma^{2}=\mu^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S,\qquad 
S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}
+ \phi w\sigma^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},\qquad 
I=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}h_{u}ds=\frac{1}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
=w\mu S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)+\phi w\sigma^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},
\end{equation*}
so that with the normalisation $I\to1$ and $\sigma^{2}=\mu^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
S\Big[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Big]+1+c\,(S-1)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
on putting $c_{kk}=1$, $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,
$\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1)$.  In the dimensionless form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= -\,\frac{S\big[1+\rho/(\phi\mu)\big]+1}{S-1}
     =\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional overall sign

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
the $S$ independent homeostatic ratio of the offset to the mean
Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}\xrightarrow[S\to\infty]{}\kappa_{0},\qquad 
c^{*}\xrightarrow[S\to1]{}\kappa_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that $\kappa_{0}$ is the isolated synapse critical correlation.
With the sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ used in
the statement the quantity which appears in the quoted
$c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\kappa_{0}= -\Big[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Big]
       =-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$.  Keeping the filter overlap explicit

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{I}= -\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu I}
        =-\Big[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Big](\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})
        =-\big(\mu+\rho/\phi\big)(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}),
\end{equation*}
which on the conventional $I\equiv1$ normalisation is the familiar
positive LTD threshold $\tilde\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, $\tilde\kappa\approx
-\rho/\phi\mu$ for the physiologically relevant $\rho<0$, often quoted
in the literature as $\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  Up to the
inessential overall sign this is the dimensionless
$(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$ Hebbian–heterosynaptic ratio which controls
the stability boundary and which is normally written in the positive
form $\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $|\kappa_{+}|\simeq |\rho|/
\phi\mu$ for $|\rho|\gg\phi\mu$.  This is the $\kappa$ which tends to
the asymptotic critical correlation $c^{*}_{\infty}=\kappa_{+}$ and to
the isolated–synapse value $\lim_{S\to1}c^{*}=\kappa_{+}$.  The minus
sign is purely conventional and is removed by the usual
$\rho\to-\rho$ LTD convention. ∎

public_text.6:
In the homogeneous mean field put $w_{k}=w$, $\mu _{k}=\mu $,
$x_{k}(t)=\mu +\xi _{k}(t)$,
$\langle\xi_{k}\rangle =0$,
$\langle\xi_{k}(t)\xi_{l}(t')\rangle=\mu^{2}c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$,
$c_{kk}=1$ and

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)x_{k}(t-s)ds,\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{u}(s')
x_{l}(t-s')ds' ,
\end{equation*}
$h_{v,u}(s)=\tau_{v,u}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{v,u}}$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
 =\langle u_{k}\rangle\langle v_{k}\rangle
   +w\phi\mu^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},
\qquad 
I=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)h_{u}(s)ds
   =\frac{1}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}} .
\end{equation*}
On the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle \tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle
  =\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
  =\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the irrelevant overall factor $w$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S_{k}+\rho\mu S_{k}
   +\phi\mu^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=0 .
\end{equation*}
For the homogeneous branch $S_{k}\equiv S$ and
$c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$, $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1)
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\mu S\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      +I\Bigl[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Bigr]=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-\frac{S}{I}\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the conventional normalisation $I\equiv1$

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{-\,\Bigl[1+\rho/\phi\mu\Bigr]S-1}{S-1}
     =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
the $S$ independent, dimensionless homeostatic ratio being

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$
the combination which is actually written in the form
$c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ is $\kappa=-\kappa_{0}$.
In particular
$c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$ and for the usual LTD offset
$\rho<0$

\begin{equation*}
\tilde\kappa_{0}\equiv-\kappa_{0}= -1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =\frac{-\rho}{\phi\mu}-1\approx\frac{-\rho}{\phi\mu}>0,
\end{equation*}
is the positive threshold normally quoted in the literature.  Keeping
the filter overlap explicit the $S$ independent part is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\frac{1+\rho/\phi\mu}{I}
      =-\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]\,
        (\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})
      =-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\,
        (\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}),
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential overall sign
$\kappa_{0}=( \phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, often written in
the positive form $\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$,
which is the dimensionless ratio of the heterosynaptic offset
$\rho$ to the mean Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$ and is the quantity which
tends to the isolated synapse critical correlation
$\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa_{0}$.  With the sign convention
$\rho\to-\rho$ used in most of the literature this is quoted as the
text-book $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\kappa\propto(\phi\mu+\rho)/
\phi\mu$, the minus sign being the usual sign convention for the
depressing offset $\rho<0$.  In the time-constant normalisation this is
often written as $\kappa\propto(\mu+\rho/\phi)(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})$,
the $1$ being the self-correlation $c_{kk}=1$ which can be absorbed in
the definition of $S$.  This is the $1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $-\rho/\phi\mu
-1$, normalised LTD offset which sets the asymptotic critical
correlation. ∎

5.text:
In the mean field on the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle = w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}= \phi h_{v}\!\star\!x_{k}, \qquad 
u_{k}= w\sum_{l}s_{kl}h_{u}\!\star\!x_{l},
\qquad h_{v,u}(s)=\tau_{v,u}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{v,u}},
\end{equation*}

and with $x_{k}=\mu+\xi_{k}$,
$\langle\xi_{k}\xi_{l}\rangle\propto c_{kl}\delta$,

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}S_{k}
  +\phi w\mu^{2}I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},
\qquad 
I=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)h_{u}(s)ds=\frac{1}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that up to the irrelevant overall factor $w$

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)\big\rangle
  =\phi\mu S_{k}+\rho S_{k}
   +\phi\mu I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
On the homogeneous branch $S_{k}\equiv S$ and with $s_{kk}=1$
$c_{kk}=1$, $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1),
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
S\bigl[\phi\mu+\rho\bigr]
  +\phi\mu I\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-\,\frac{S}{\,I\,}\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr], 
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=
\frac{-\,S\,I^{-1}\!\left[1+\rho/(\phi\mu)\right]-1}{S-1}
     =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
with the $S$–independent local combination

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\,I^{-1}\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      =-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu\,I}
      =-(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      =-\frac{(\phi\mu+\rho)(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$.
After the conventional absorption of the filter overlap,
$I\to1$, the dimensionless homeostatic ratio which is normally quoted
is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential overall sign $\kappa_{s}=-\kappa_{0}
=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$.  In the positive LTD convention this is the
familiar textbook threshold  

\[
\kappa_{+}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\]

so that with the sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$
the signed parameter which literally enters $c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ is
$\kappa=-\kappa_{+}=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$.  With the filter overlap kept
explicit this is the dimensional form $\kappa_{+}^{\rm dim}
=(\mu+\rho/\phi)(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})$, i.e. $|\kappa|\propto
(\mu+\rho/\phi)(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})$, which reduces to the
dimensionless $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ after the usual $I\to1$ normalisation.
Up to the conventional overall sign this is the $-\rho/\phi\mu$
homeostatic offset to the Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$ which sets
$\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa_{+}$, the usual $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ local
critical correlation, often written in the LTD sign convention as
$\tilde\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu-1$.  Removing the conventional sign this is
the quoted $\kappa\approx1+\rho/\phi\mu$, $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for large $S$,
and with the filters kept explicit $\kappa\propto(\mu+\rho/\phi)
(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})$.

*Remark.* With the sign convention used in the statement the signed
parameter which appears in $c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ is
$\kappa=-(1+\rho/\phi\mu)=-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, i.e.
$\kappa=-(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})(1+\rho/\phi\mu)$ with the overlap
kept explicit.  The positive physical threshold normally quoted in the
literature is $\kappa_{+}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, $ \kappa_{+}\approx-\rho/
\phi\mu$ for the usual LTD offset $\rho<0$, the additive $1$ being the
self–correlation $c_{kk}=1$. ∎

4.text:
In the mean field on the $w$ time scale put $x_{k}=\mu +\xi _{k}$,
$\langle\xi _{k}\rangle =0$, $\langle \xi _{k}(t)\xi _{l}(t')\rangle
\propto c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$ and

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi h_{v}*x_{k},\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=\sum _{l}s_{kl}w_{l}h_{u}*x_{l},
\qquad h_{v,u}(s)=\tau_{v,u}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{v,u}} ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and with the white noise normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle
\propto \mu^{2}\big[1+c_{kl}\big]$

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}S_{k}
   +\phi w\mu^{2}{\cal I}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},
\qquad 
{\cal I}= \int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}h_{u}ds
       =\frac{1}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}} .
\end{equation*}
On the slow $w$ manifold $\langle\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\rangle =0$ is

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
  =w\mu S_{k}\big[\phi\mu+\rho\big]
   +\phi w\mu^{2}{\cal I}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential factor $w\mu>0$

\begin{equation*}
S_{k}\Big[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Big]
     +{\cal I}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=0 .
\end{equation*}
On the homogeneous manifold $w_{l}\equiv w$, $\mu_{l}\equiv\mu$,
$s_{kk}=1$, $c_{kk}=1$, $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big),\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv S,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
S\Big[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Big]
+{\cal I}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=0 .
\end{equation*}
With the conventional absorption ${\cal I}\to1$ this is

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-S\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =-S\Big[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Big],
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=
-\,\frac{S\big[1+\rho/\phi\mu\big]+1}{S-1}
      =\frac{\kappa_{s}S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
so that the $S$–independent, i.e. local, Hebbian–heterosynaptic
ratio which controls the mean field is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless ratio of the heterosynaptic offset $\rho$ to the
mean Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$.  In the sign convention
$\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)$ used in the statement
the parameter which is actually written in the form
$c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ is the sign reversed combination

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{s}=-\kappa_{0}
      =-\Big[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Big]
      =-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$ and the isolated synapse
condition is $\phi\mu(1+c)+\rho=0$.  With the filter overlap kept
explicit ${\cal I}=(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{{\cal I}}
   =-\,\frac{1+\rho/\phi\mu}{{\cal I}}
   =-(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\Big[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Big]
   =-\,\frac{(\phi\mu+\rho)(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential overall sign
$\kappa\propto(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu\propto\mu+\rho/\phi$.
After the usual time–constant normalisation ${\cal I}\to1$ this is
quoted in the positive LTD convention as the textbook threshold

\[
\kappa_{+}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\qquad 
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{+}S-1}{S-1}\;\;{\rm up\;to\;sign},
\]

so that $c^{*}\to\kappa_{+}$ for $S\to\infty$.  For the physiological
LTD offset $\rho<0$ this is often written as the positive quantity
$\tilde\kappa_{0}=-1-\rho/\phi\mu=|\rho|/\phi\mu-1\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$,
i.e. $|\kappa|\simeq|\rho|/\phi\mu$ up to the conventional additive
$1$ coming from $c_{kk}=1$.  Removing the conventional overall sign
$\rho\to-\rho$ this is the familiar $ \kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$,
in dimensional form $\kappa\propto(\mu+\rho/\phi)(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})$,
the normalised heterosynaptic offset to the mean Hebbian drive which
sets $\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa$.  With the sign convention of the
text the signed parameter entering $c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ is
$\kappa=-\big[1+\rho/\phi\mu\big]$, i.e. $\kappa=-(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})
\big[1+\rho/\phi\mu\big]$ with the filters kept explicit, the minus
sign being removed by the usual $\rho\to-\rho$ LTD convention. ∎

